    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d695">

                <pb facs="0829" n="543" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg543"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-01-05T16:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 5, 1917, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4250. Your 5290, December 7, 4 p. m. Since you have modified the character of the
                    representations which the Department outlined in its 4117, December 2,
                    Department hopes you will make particular efforts to obtain favorable action as
                    to shipments of seed still detained in Rotterdam as to which you state in your
                    5290 you made further representations. Great difficulty and expense is being
                    caused by indefinite delay in this case and you will make plain to Foreign
                    Office that as importers acted in good faith upon information given them by
                    Department based upon your 3760 February 11,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d695-f1"/>
                    Department’s good faith in matter is involved and under circumstances it has
                    grounds to expect favorable and prompt action as to these two shipments, the
                    Department being willing if this is granted to leave question of further
                    shipments of seed to future discussion and not to regard action of British
                    authorities in this case as a precedent.</p>
                <p>In order to impress British Government with importance of this matter the
                    question of these shipments was taken up with British Embassy on November 14 and
                        Crawford seemed to understand the
                    justice of our contention. Embassy transmitted memorandum <hi
                        rendition="#italic">re</hi> matter to Foreign Office which replied that it
                    thought advisable that discussion of question be continued through you rather
                    than through Embassy here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
